WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.666
Last video for week three.

2
00:00:01.716 --> 00:00:08.530
Two topics that both answer the same question: what does the agent know that is not in this turn's context?

3
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.932
Memory is what it keeps between runs.

4
00:00:10.982 --> 00:00:14.708
Skills are instructions it loads when a task calls for them.

5
00:00:14.758 --> 00:00:18.483
Both are harness design, and both are easy to get wrong.

6
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:20.895
Start with the two kinds.

7
00:00:20.945 --> 00:00:26.032
Session memory is everything in the current run: the goal, the history, the tool results.

8
00:00:26.082 --> 00:00:33.566
It lives in the context window and it ends when the run ends, unless the harness compacts it or writes it down.

9
00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:36.649
Long-term memory is what the agent keeps between runs.

10
00:00:36.699 --> 00:00:43.840
Some of it is facts: this customer prefers email, this decision was made on Tuesday, these three items are still open.

11
00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:48.977
Some of it is procedure: the way a task was done last time that worked.

12
00:00:49.027 --> 00:00:56.511
Long-term memory is stored outside the model, in a file or a database, and retrieved into the context when it is relevant.

13
00:00:56.561 --> 00:01:00.963
Which means it is retrieval, with everything the last video said about failure.

14
00:01:01.713 --> 00:01:11.653
Anthropic published a piece on the components of agent memory in November 2026, one sign that this has become an engineering topic rather than a research one.

15
00:01:11.703 --> 00:01:14.612
The division of labor to remember is this.

16
00:01:14.662 --> 00:01:17.572
The agent decides what is worth writing down.

17
00:01:17.622 --> 00:01:24.232
The harness decides where it goes, who can read it, and when it comes back into the context.

18
00:01:24.282 --> 00:01:30.151
And every remembered item is a cost: tokens on every later turn in which it appears.

19
00:01:30.201 --> 00:01:34.591
A memory that remembers everything is a context problem waiting to happen.

20
00:01:35.341 --> 00:01:37.066
Four failures to design against.

21
00:01:37.116 --> 00:01:42.392
Stale memory: the fact was true in March and the agent still acts on it.

22
00:01:42.442 --> 00:01:48.429
Wrong scope: one user's preference applied to another user, which in a company is a privacy incident.

23
00:01:48.479 --> 00:01:56.240
Poisoned memory: an instruction that arrived inside a document got saved as a fact, and now it comes back on every run.

24
00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:00.856
And unexplained memory: nobody can see why the agent believes what it believes.

25
00:02:00.906 --> 00:02:09.733
The remedies are the same in every case: timestamps, scope by user and team, a store a person can read, and a way to delete.

26
00:02:10.483 --> 00:02:11.157
Now skills.

27
00:02:11.207 --> 00:02:16.587
A skill is a folder: a file of instructions, plus optional scripts and reference material.

28
00:02:16.637 --> 00:02:25.275
The agent sees only a short description of each skill up front, and loads the full instructions into the context when the task matches.

29
00:02:25.325 --> 00:02:30.705
Anthropic introduced the format in October 2025 and it has spread across agent products since.

30
00:02:30.755 --> 00:02:33.239
Why does it matter for a service?

31
00:02:33.289 --> 00:02:35.049
Because expertise becomes a file.

32
00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:38.307
It can be reviewed, versioned and tested like code.

33
00:02:38.357 --> 00:02:44.099
The system prompt stays short, which the first video this week told you to care about.

34
00:02:44.149 --> 00:02:51.701
And a skill does not require programming, so the product owner or the domain expert on your team can write one.

35
00:02:52.451 --> 00:02:54.059
Two things for this week.

36
00:02:54.109 --> 00:03:00.357
Write a memory design for your agent: what is stored, where, with what scope, and how it is deleted.

37
00:03:00.407 --> 00:03:01.683
One page is enough.

38
00:03:01.733 --> 00:03:08.644
And write one skill for your agent's job, then run it on three tasks and compare with the runs without it.

39
00:03:08.694 --> 00:03:13.947
In the proposal, add one sentence that most teams forget: what the agent must never remember.

40
00:03:13.997 --> 00:03:15.273
Next week we measure.

41
00:03:15.323 --> 00:03:18.588
Golden sets, pass^k, and reading traces properly.
